<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>Some questions<br />point at a picture.</h2><p>“What is unusual about this scene?” is an incomplete question if the model only receives those words. You could describe every detail yourself, but then you have already done much of the work.</p><p>A chart, a photographed page, or a sketch carries information that a text-only conversation cannot directly supply.</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Words and pictures in.<br />Words out.</h2><p>OpenAI introduced GPT-4 in March 2023. The report described a <strong>multimodal</strong> model: one that can work with more than one kind of input. GPT-4 could accept images alongside text and produce a text response.</p><p>The paper showed examples involving photographs, diagrams and documents. A question could refer to something visible, rather than requiring the user to translate the whole image into prose first.</p><p>The broad training recipe still involved predicting the next <strong>token</strong>, a piece of text, followed by training to improve behaviour. The report did not reveal the detailed image-processing architecture. We can explain what visual input makes possible without inventing how GPT-4 encoded it.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram vision-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 400 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="vision-title vision-desc"><title id="vision-title">Text and image provide evidence for one answer</title><desc id="vision-desc">A question asking how many circles joins an image of two circles and a square. Both flow into the model, which returns text. This is an invented illustration.</desc><rect x="10" y="10" width="380" height="65" rx="5" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--line)"/><text x="200" y="50" text-anchor="middle" font-size="25" fill="currentColor">“How many circles?”</text><text x="200" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26" fill="currentColor">+</text><rect x="65" y="125" width="270" height="75" rx="5" fill="var(--accent)"/><g fill="var(--ink)"><circle cx="125" cy="162" r="21"/><rect x="179" y="141" width="42" height="42"/><circle cx="275" cy="162" r="21"/></g><g text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor"><text x="200" y="237" font-size="30">↓</text><text x="200" y="280" font-size="25">Model reads both inputs</text><text x="200" y="317" font-size="30">↓</text><text x="200" y="353" font-size="25">“Two circles.”</text></g></svg><figcaption>Input and output, not an architecture diagram. The shapes and answer are invented.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>What’s in<br />the picture?</h2><Discovery slug="gpt-4" /><p>Uncover a drawing one tile at a time and name it as early as you dare: fewer tiles earn more points. A text-only model plays too, but it only gets the caption. Three pictures.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to uncover the tiles. You see the picture; the text-only model sees only its caption.</p></noscript><VisionToy client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>The prompt can<br />point beyond prose.</h2><p>A picture can now supply part of the question. The report’s examples illustrate a broader interface for explaining documents and reasoning about visual scenes. They do not prove reliable performance on every image.</p><p>Adding input types is one way to expand a model’s usefulness. Another question is how to steer what it says. Next, a simpler way to train a model on the answers people prefer.</p></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>What the report reveals—and leaves open</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>A conditional text generator</h3><p>A useful abstraction is <code>P(y | x, I) = ∏t P(yt | x, I, y&lt;t)</code>: predict reply tokens y using text x, image I and earlier reply tokens. This does not specify how images become internal representations. The report identifies GPT-4 as Transformer-based, but withholds model size, detailed architecture, training compute and dataset construction. In particular, it does not establish a specific vision encoder or mixture-of-experts design.</p><h3>A headline result, with its limits</h3><p>The report places GPT-4 around the top 10% on a simulated bar examination, compared with GPT-3.5 around the bottom 10%. This is a result on a particular exam setup, not a measure of general intelligence or a qualification to practise law. The image examples and the exam evaluation are different evidence; the exam result alone does not measure visual understanding.</p><h3>Capability and availability</h3><p>At the March 2023 announcement, image input was a research preview and was not publicly available. The multimodal capability described in the report should not be confused with what every user could access on launch day.</p><h3>Still a fallible reader</h3><p>The report describes fabricated answers, reasoning errors and overconfidence. Its visual demonstrations are selected examples. Our toy is deliberately simpler. You are the one looking at the pixels; the text-only model receives only the caption and picks the choice whose usual words overlap it most. It does not perform computer vision or simulate GPT-4.</p><p>A correct name scores nine minus the tiles you opened, so one tile earns 8 and a wrong name earns 0. Each caption is true but vague, which is exactly the situation where words alone leave the question open and the image carries the answer.</p></div></details>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="sources"><summary>Sources <span>Technical report and official launch</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><ol class="source-list"><li><a href={paperLink("gpt-4-report")}>OpenAI · GPT-4 Technical Report · 2023</a><p>Abstract and Sections 1–2: image-and-text input, text output, next-token training, Transformer basis and omitted implementation details. Section 4 and Table 1: simulated exam evaluation. Section 4’s visual-input examples: photographs, diagrams and documents. Section 5: reasoning errors, hallucinations, limited context and calibration.</p></li><li><a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-4-research/">OpenAI · GPT-4 · March 14, 2023</a><p>Launch date, visual-input examples, and the statement that image inputs were a research preview and not publicly available. The limitations section explains why capability demonstrations do not guarantee reliable use.</p></li><li><a href={sourceLink("ouyang-2022-neurips-full-text")}>Ouyang et al. · Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback · 2022</a><p>Background for the behaviour-training approach referenced by the GPT-4 report.</p></li></ol><p>The drawings, diagram and caption-matching rule are original teaching illustrations. They are not GPT-4 outputs or measurements.</p></div></details>
